(globalThis.TURBOPACK||(globalThis.TURBOPACK=[])).push(["object"==typeof document?document.currentScript:void 0,18741,e=>{e.v({print:"ReflectionBlock-module__g-ZaYW__print",reflectionCard:"ReflectionBlock-module__g-ZaYW__reflectionCard"})},3596,94857,e=>{"use strict";let a={slug:"verse-0",title:"Durga Mantra",description:"Maa Durga - The Feminine Energy which will protect us always",sanskrit:`
      ॐ दुं दुर्गायै नमः 
      या देवी सर्वभू‍तेषु माँ कूष्माण्डा रूपेण  
      संस्थिता। नमस्तस्यै नमस्तस्यै नमस्तस्यै नमो नमः॥ 
`,meaning:["Om Dum Durgayei Namaha (ॐ दुं दुर्गायै नमः) is a powerful Sanskrit mantra that honors Goddess Durga, the Divine Mother, and invokes her divine protection","Ya Devi Sarvabhuteshu Maa Kushmanda Rupena Samsthita। Namastasyai Namastasyai Namastasyai Namo Namah॥"],symbols:["[TITLE] Make Maa Durga Your Anchor","[HEADER] Overcoming the Demons Through Devotion","[PARAGRAPH] In the scriptural narrative, the demons Madhu (मधु) and Kaitabha (कैटभ) arise from the impurity (मल, mala) associated with the ear of Lord Vishnu (विष्णु) while he rests in yogic sleep (योगनिद्रा, yoganidrā). When Brahma (ब्रह्मा), representing awakened intelligence or discriminative intellect (बुद्धि, buddhi), invokes the Divine Mother, Mahāmāyā (महामाया) withdraws her power of sleep from Vishnu, enabling him to confront the demons. Yet even after a prolonged battle, they cannot be defeated until Mahāmāyā herself deludes them with pride and self-conceit (अहंकार, ahaṃkāra).","[PARAGRAPH] Philosophically, this can be understood as an allegory of the inner spiritual struggle. The Tamas (तमस्) of ignorance and delusion gives rise to Rāga (राग), or attachment, symbolized by Madhu (मधु). Likewise, the Rajas (रजस्) of restlessness and agitation gives rise to Dveṣa (द्वेष), or aversion, symbolized by Kaitabha (कैटभ). Similarly, Mahishasura (महिषासुर) symbolizes the powerful and shape-shifting ego (अहंकार, ahaṃkāra) that continually reasserts itself.","[PARAGRAPH] The deeper lesson is that attachment (राग, rāga), aversion (द्वेष, dveṣa), and ego (अहंकार, ahaṃkāra) cannot be conquered merely by the ordinary mind acting through its own limited power. Intellectual understanding (बुद्धि, buddhi) alone is insufficient. Liberation requires surrender to the Divine Energy (शक्ति, śakti), from which arises Vairāgya (वैराग्य), or true detachment. Through Vairāgya (वैराग्य), one gains the clarity to see beyond Māyā (माया), the veiling power that causes the impermanent to appear permanent and the unreal to appear real. Only then can the forces of attachment (राग, rāga), aversion (द्वेष, dveṣa), and ego (अहंकार, ahaṃkāra) be fully dissolved.","[INTRO] Three-Step Shift in Focus: A practical method to allow the Divine Energy (शक्ति, Śakti) to anchor within oneself.","[HEADER] 1. ACKNOWLEDGE: Catching the Demons","[BULLET] Catching the Demons: When chaos arises, your immediate reaction is often driven by Madhu (मधु)—representing Attachment (राग, Rāga), the urge to cling to how things should be—or Kaitabha (कैटभ)—representing Aversion (द्वेष, Dveṣa), the impulse to resist and fight against what is.","[BULLET] The Practice: Do not suppress the anxiety, fear, or emotional turbulence.",'[BULLET] The Pivot: Simply name it. Say to yourself, "The storm is here. My Ego (अहंकार, Ahaṃkāra) is reacting." This act of recognition creates a small but powerful distance between you and the emotion, preventing the demons from hijacking your Intellect (बुद्धि, Buddhi), symbolized by Brahma (ब्रह्मा).',"[HEADER] 2. INVOKE: Activating the Matrix (Durga)","[BULLET] Activating the Matrix: In the myth, Brahma (ब्रह्मा) invokes Mahāmāyā (महामाया), the Divine Mother Durga (दुर्गा), to awaken Vishnu (विष्णु) from Yogic Sleep (योगनिद्रा, Yoganidrā). Internally, you are performing the same act—calling upon Divine Energy (शक्ति, Śakti) to awaken from unconscious reactivity.","[BULLET] The Practice: Use a deliberate pattern interrupter.",'[BULLET] The Pivot: Take one deep breath and consciously align with your highest nature. If you use a mantra such as "ॐ दुं दुर्गायै नमः," allow it to feel like a protective shield settling into place. If you ask, "What would my highest self do?", let the answer arise from clarity, steadiness, and conviction.',"[HEADER] 3. AWARENESS: Becoming the Silent Witness (Sākṣī)","[BULLET] Becoming the Silent Witness: Once Durga (दुर्गा) stabilizes the inner field, Vishnu (विष्णु) awakens, symbolizing the all-pervading Consciousness (चैतन्य, Caitanya) that sustains existence. Resting as the Witness (साक्षी, Sākṣī), you begin to abide in that Vishnu-consciousness.","[BULLET] The Practice: Observe the anxiety, fear, or problem as though you are watching it from the outside.","[BULLET] The Pivot: Realize that the storm is occurring within the Mind (मनस्, Manas), but it is not occurring to your true Self (आत्मन्, Ātman). As the Witness (साक्षी, Sākṣī), you discover that the storm has no power to damage the sky through which it passes. Through the grace of Divine Energy (शक्ति, Śakti), Attachment (राग, Rāga) and Aversion (द्वेष, Dveṣa) gradually dissolve, giving rise to Detachment (वैराग्य, Vairāgya) and freedom from the illusions of Māyā (माया)."],philosophy:["This interpretation reflects my personal spiritual journey. I began with devotional worship of Maa Durga and later came to appreciate the symbolic and psychological dimensions of the tradition. I do not see these perspectives as contradictory; for me, devotion and inner inquiry illuminate one another."],reflection:["The demons destroyed by Durga Maa symbolize the destructive tendencies within us—fear, cowardice,  greed, hatred, arrogance, self-doubt, and other forces that weaken our strength, courage, and inner clarity.","Durga's fierce compassion, often expressed as righteous or protective anger,   is the battle inside us in daily life where this compassionate rage confronts  and destroys the inner demons before they can consume us.   ","This interpretation reflects my personal spiritual journey. I began with devotional worship of Maa Durga and later came to appreciate the symbolic and psychological dimensions of the tradition. I do not see these perspectives as contradictory; for me, devotion and inner inquiry illuminate one another."],image1:"/images/maa/maadurgamantra.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/pntmantra.png",audio:"/audio/maverse1.wav"},t={slug:"verse-1",title:"Verse 1 : अयि गिरिनन्दिनि",description:"1.	Maa Durga - The Daughter of the Mountain and Joy of the World",sanskrit:`
      अयि गिरिनन्दिनि नन्दितमेदिनि विश्वविनोदिनि नन्दिनुते
      गिरिवरविन्ध्यशिरोऽधिनिवासिनि विष्णुविलासिनि जिष्णुनुते ।
      भगवति हे शितिकण्ठकुटुम्बिनि भूरिकुटुम्बिनि भूरिकृते
      जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥ १ ॥
                      
`,meaning:['1.1: <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="अयि">Salutations to You</span> O Divine Mother, I Invoke You; Who is the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="गिरिनन्दिनि">Daughter of the Mountain</span>; By Whose presence <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="नन्दितमेदिनि">the whole World is filled with Joy</span>; For Whom the whole <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="विश्वविनोदिनि">World is a Divine Play</span> and Who is <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="नन्दिनुते">Praised by Nandi</span>,','1.2: (I Invoke You O Devi) Who, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शिरोऽधिनिवासिनि">resides on the peak of the mountain range</span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="गिरिवरविन्ध्य">Vindhya Mountains </span>; Who gives <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="विष्णुविलासिनि">Joy to Lord Vishnu</span> (as His sister) and Who is <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जिष्णुनुते">Praised by Lord Indra</span>,','1.3: <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="भगवति">O Goddess Bhagavati</span>, Who is the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शितिकण्ठकुटुम्बिनि">Consort of the One with Blue Throat</span> (Lord Shiva); Who has <span class="highlight"  data-sanskrit="भूरिकुटुम्बिनि ">Many Many Relations</span> in this World (being the Cosmic Mother) and Who has <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="भूरिकृते">Created Abundance</span> (in Creation),','1.4: Victory to You, Victory to You, (I take Refuge in Your Auspicious Feet) O the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="महिषासुरमर्दिनि">Destroyer of Demon Mahishasura</span>; (Victory to You) Who Shine with <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि">Beautiful Locks of Hair</span> and Who is the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते">Daughter of the Mountain</span>.'],symbols:['Daughter of the Mountain (Shailaputri): Durga as Giri-Nandini, born of the Himalayas. As the daughter of the earth, she grounds spiritual awakening and symbolizes the strength, patience, and purity of nature. The iconic hymn Mahishasura Mardini Stotram beautifully addresses her as "Giri - Nandini" (daughter of the mountain)',"Yogamaya / Vishnumaya: She is Vishnu’s illusionary, cosmic power. She creates the Maya that keeps the universe running and aids Vishnu in his earthly avatars","Sister: During the birth of Lord Krishna (Vishnu's avatar), she incarnated as the daughter of Yashoda and Nanda (the sibling of Krishna). In this form, she is recognized as Vishnu-Durga or Narayani, the ultimate protector.","Nandi: As the first manifestation (Shailaputri), she rides the bull Nandi, which is Shiva's mount. Symbolically, Nandi represents faith and pure devotion; the Goddess embodies the supreme consciousness riding upon unwavering devotion","Indra: In myths surrounding the destruction of the demon Mahishasura, the gods (including the king of heaven, Indra) were powerless. They pooled their individual powers (Shakti) to create Durga. Indra gifted her his thunderbolt (Vajra), representing his ultimate warrior strength."],philosophy:["This verse invokes Maa Durga as the ultimate source of cosmic bliss and divine strength who dwells majestically on the summits of the Vindhya mountains alongside Shiva, Vishnu, Nandi and Indra. By slaying the buffalo-demon Mahishasura, She symbolizes the ultimate victory of divine intellect and spiritual awakening over human ego, laziness, and ignorance."],reflection:["Goddess Durga represents the supreme, invincible energy of the universe (Shakti). Her deeper meaning bridges the gap between the material and spiritual realms: She is the force of creation, the mother who grounds humanity, and the ultimate warrior who destroys the ego and inner ignorance"],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse1.png",image2:"/images/maa/arthverse1.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/pntverse1.png",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"अयि",meaning:"O! / Hail!"},{sanskrit:"गिरिनन्दिनि",meaning:"Daughter of the mountain (Himalaya)"},{sanskrit:"नन्दितमेदिनि",meaning:"She who delights the earth"},{sanskrit:"विश्वविनोदिनि",meaning:"She who brings joy to the universe"},{sanskrit:"नन्दिनुते",meaning:"Praised by Nandi"},{sanskrit:"गिरिवरविन्ध्यशिरोऽधिनिवासिनि",meaning:"She who dwells on the peaks of the great Vindhya mountains"},{sanskrit:"विष्णुविलासिनि",meaning:"She who reveals the creative, joyful power of Vishnu"},{sanskrit:"जिष्णुनुते",meaning:"Praised by the victorious ones (or Indra)"},{sanskrit:"भगवति",meaning:"O Goddess! (Possessor of all divine opulences)"},{sanskrit:"हे",meaning:"O!"},{sanskrit:"शितिकण्ठकुटुम्बिनि",meaning:"Consort of the blue-throated one (Shiva)"},{sanskrit:"भूरिकुटुम्बिनि",meaning:"She who has the entire cosmos as Her abundant family"},{sanskrit:"भूरिकृते",meaning:"She who creates abundantly / brings forth countless blessings"},{sanskrit:"जय",meaning:"Victory!"},{sanskrit:"जय",meaning:"Victory!"},{sanskrit:"हे",meaning:"O!"},{sanskrit:"महिषासुरमर्दिनि",meaning:"Destroyer of the demon Mahishasura"},{sanskrit:"रम्यकपर्दिनि",meaning:"She with beautiful, intricately woven locks of hair"},{sanskrit:"शैलसुते",meaning:"Daughter of the mountain"}],audio:"/audio/averse1.wav"},n={slug:"verse-2",title:"Verse 2 : सुरवरवर्षिणि",description:"2. Maa Durga - Destroys Durdhara, Durumuka, Danavas ,Daityas    ",sanskrit:`
    सुरवरवर्षिणि दुर्धरधर्षिणि दुर्मुखमर्षिणि हर्षरते
    त्रिभुवनपोषिणि शङ्करतोषिणि किल्बिषमोषिणि घोषरते
    दनुजनिरोषिणि दितिसुतरोषिणि दुर्मदशोषिणि सिन्धुसुते
    जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥ २ ॥
                      
`,meaning:['<strong>2.1:</strong>  O Divine Mother Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="सुरवरवर्षिणि">Showers Boons on the Devas</span>; Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दुर्धरधर्षिणि">Overpowered Demon Durdhara</span> (Unrestrainable) and <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दुर्मुखमर्षिणि">Endured Demon Durmukha</span> (Foul-Mouthed) and finally slayed him, and Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="हर्षरते">Delight in Her own Bliss</span>.<br />',"<br /><strong>Psychological:</strong> You are the force that showers divine blessings upon higher mental virtues, yet You turn into the unyielding power that shatters our insatiable desires. While You are the destroyer of corrosive, foul-mouthed anger, You remain eternally anchored in supreme, unshakeable bliss even amidst the thick of our inner psychological battles.",'<strong>2.2:</strong> Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="त्रिभुवनपोषिणि">Sustain and Nourish the Three-Worlds</span>; Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शङ्करतोषिणि">Delight Lord Shankara</span> by removing sins by <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="किल्बिषमोषिणि">Wiping away Deep-Seated Impurities</span>; Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="घोषरते">Delight in the Roar of Battle</span>.<br />',"<br /><strong>Psychological:</strong> You are the dynamic sustainer who nourishes our physical, mental, and spiritual worlds, working beautifully as the energy that brings complete joy to silent, pure consciousness. You act as the absolute purifier of toxic mental impressions, maintaining a triumphant presence that welcomes the roaring challenges of life with absolute confidence.",'<strong>2.3:</strong> Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दनुजनिरोषिणि">Quell the Wrath of the Danavas</span>   and is <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दितिसुतरोषिणि">Angry with the Daityas</span>  ; Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दुर्मदशोषिणि">Dry up the Foolish Pride of Demons</span>; and Who is the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="सिन्धुसुते">Daughter of the Ocean</span>.<br />',"<br /><strong>Psychological:</strong> You are the subduer of heavy, stagnant ignorance and the fierce challenger of the rigid, divisive ego, working constantly to dry up the toxic, foolish pride of the human mind. As the Daughter of the Ocean, You emerge from the deep churn of our awareness to grant boundless prosperity and ultimate liberation.",'<strong>2.4:</strong> <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जय जय हे">Victory to You</span>, O Mother the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="महिषासुरमर्दिनि">Destroyer of Demon Mahishasura</span>;  Who Shines with <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि">Beautiful Locks of Hair</span> and Who is the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते">Daughter of the Mountain</span>.<br />',"<br /><strong>Psychological:</strong> Victory, victory to You, O Destroyer of the monstrous delusion of the ego, You whose beautiful braided locks hold the complex forces of the cosmos in perfect order, and who shines as the Daughter of the Mountain of steady, unwavering meditation."],symbols:["[TITLE] Cosmological, Elemental, and Spiritual Symbolism","[INTRO] In Hindu philosophy and mythology, the elements of earth, water, and sky represent the material layers of creation, while Goddess Durga symbolizes the absolute, unified divine energy (<em>Adi Parashakti</em>) that governs, protects, and transcends them all. When viewed together, this combination carries profound cosmological, elemental, and spiritual symbolism.","[HEADER] 1. The Matrix of Material Creation (Pancha Mahabhuta)","[BULLET] Water (Jala): Symbolizes fluidity, the primordial soup of creation, emotion, and the preservation of life. In the context of the Danavas, it represents the untamed depths of the subconscious and unmanifested potential.","[BULLET] Earth (Prithvi): Symbolizes stability, structure, the physical body, and the ultimate crystallization of consciousness into matter. For the Daityas, it represents ego, raw material power, and worldly ambition.","[BULLET] Sky (Akasha): Symbolizes vastness, space, infinite consciousness, and the spiritual realm. Connected to Aditi and the Devas, it represents higher wisdom, light, and the ultimate boundaries of order.","[BULLET] Agni (Fire): Symbolizes transformation, metabolism, and inner power, aligning with Durga’s fierce, energy-driven, and transformative warrior nature.","[BULLET] Vayu (Air): Represents movement, breath, and transportation, corresponding to the vital life force (Prana) that the Goddess grants to creation.","[HEADER] 2. Goddess Durga as the Cosmic Synthesis","[PARAGRAPH] <strong>The Birthing of Durga:</strong> According to the Devi Mahatmya, Durga was created when the individual energies (<em>Tejas</em>) of all the gods fused into one blazing mountain of light. Her physical form directly incorporates the elements: her face is the light of Shiva (pure consciousness/sky), her legs and thighs come from Varuna (water), and her hips are formed from Bhoodevi (earth).","[PARAGRAPH] <strong>The Ultimate Protector:</strong> While individual factions (like the Danavas of water, Daityas of earth, and Adityas of the sky) fight for dominance over their specific realms, Durga is <em>Mahishasura Mardini</em>—the force that steps in when the balance between earth, water, and sky is completely shattered by chaos.","[HEADER] 3. The Sacred Geometry (The Durga Yantra)","[PARAGRAPH] This connection is perfectly captured in the <strong>Durga Yantra</strong>, a sacred geometric diagram used in rituals to invoke her presence. Within this geometry, a lotus pattern represents the five physical elements (including earth, water, and sky), while the central focal point—the <strong>Bindu</strong>—symbolizes Goddess Durga herself as the singular Supreme Consciousness from which all elements emerge and collapse back into.","[DIAGRAM] [ HIGH REALMS: SKY / ADITI ]   -> Infinite Space & Light","[DIAGRAM]                │","[DIAGRAM] [ MIDDLE REALMS: WATER / DANU ] -> Primordial Depth & Flow","[DIAGRAM]                │","[DIAGRAM] [ LOW REALMS: EARTH / DITI ]    -> Rigid Matter & Power","[DIAGRAM]                │","[DIAGRAM] ═══════════════════▼═══════════════════","[DIAGRAM] 🌟   GODDESS DURGA (Adi Parashakti)    🌟 -> Synthesizes & Balances All","[DIAGRAM] ═══════════════════════════════════════","[SOURCE] 1. Vedadhara: <a href='https://vedadhara.com' target='_blank'>\"Origin of Devas, Daityas, and Danavas\"</a> — vedadhara.com","[SOURCE] 2. Quora Community: <a href='https://quora.com' target='_blank'>\"What are differences between Aditya, Daitya, Danav...\"</a> — quora.com","[SOURCE] 3. Facebook: <a href='https://www.facebook.com/sagarworldmultimedia/posts/durga-yantra-the-sacred-geometrical-diagram-to-invoke-goddess-durgafind-the-link/2657629860962843/' target='_blank' style='color: #b45309; text-decoration: underline;'>View Original Post</a> — @sagarworldmultimedia","[SOURCE] 4. DeviMahatmya <a>https://devimahatmya.com/</a>"],philosophy:["<strong>Six Demons</strong>: <strong>(kāma)</strong> is any kind of longing for sensory gratification,wealth, or power. Inherent in it is the sense of deficiency, limitation, or separation. Any desire is ultimately the desire for wholeness. When thwarted, it may turn to anger <strong>(krodha)</strong>; paradoxically, when gratified it may become inflamed into greed <strong>(lobha)</strong>. Because the ego, by its very nature, remains deficient, no amount of gratification can fill the void. Additionally, fixation on the ego manifests as pride <strong>(mada)</strong>, a sense of superiority that in turn can engender jealousy <strong>(mātsarya)</strong>. All these entangled passions thrive in delusion <strong>(moha)</strong>, which clouds awareness and distorts self-perception.❤️"],reflection:["Philosophy","Shiva as the साक्षी  : Shiva represents the unmoving awareness that observes all mental activity without participating in it.In philosophy, Shiva is Prakasha (the unmoving light of consciousness); he is pure stillness.","Durga as the शक्ति :   Durga is Vimarsha (the dynamic, self-aware energy that acts).  Durga is the processing power of your mind that cleans up house so stillness can be experienced.","Replacing 'Brain' with Buddhi (Higher Intellect): In yogic psychology, the physical brain is just hardware. The software that fights inner battles is Buddhi—the faculty of discernment, higher intelligence, and spiritual awareness.","Quoted passage about the <strong>six demons</strong> (verbatim from <a>https://devimahatmya.com)</a>: Desire <strong>(kāma)</strong> is in the broadest sense any kind of longing for sensory gratification, material wealth, or power. Inherent in it is the sense of deficiency, limitation, or separation. Any desire is ultimately the desire for wholeness. When thwarted, it may turn to anger <strong>(krodha)</strong>; paradoxically, when gratified it may become inflamed into greed <strong>(lobha)</strong>—excessive, insatiable want. Because the ego, by its very nature, remains deficient, no amount of gratification can fill the void. Additionally, fixation on the ego manifests as pride <strong>(mada)</strong>, a sense of superiority that in turn can engender jealousy <strong>(mātsarya)</strong>—resentment of others' fortune. All these entangled passions thrive in delusion <strong>(moha)</strong>, which clouds awareness and distorts self-perception.❤️"],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse2a.png",image2:"/images/maa/arthverse2.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/pntverse2.png",symbology:"/blog/maadurga/katha/symbology/verse2",scenario:"/blog/maadurga/katha/reflection/verse2",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"सुरवरवर्षिणि",meaning:"The descent of divine grace, clarity, and higher spiritual virtues into the human intellect when the mind aligns with truth."},{sanskrit:"दुर्धरधर्षिणि",meaning:"Durdhara represents blind, unbridled, and insatiable desire (Kama) that resists all human restraint until subdued by spiritual discipline."},{sanskrit:"दुर्मुखमर्षिणि",meaning:"Durmukha represents corrosive anger (Krodha) and abusive speech. When desire (Durdhara) is blocked, it instantly mutates into rage (Durmukha)"},{sanskrit:"हर्षरते",meaning:"Absolute equanimity. The Divine Mother remains in a state of supreme bliss and detached joy even while engaging in the chaotic, heavy warfare of life."},{sanskrit:"त्रिभुवनपोषिणि",meaning:"The integration and nurturing of our physical body, conscious mind, and deep subconscious spirit under a unified divine order."},{sanskrit:"शङ्करतोषिणि",meaning:"Shiva represents pure, silent, unmoving cosmic consciousness. Devi pleasing Him symbolizes dynamic energy working in perfect harmony with silent spiritual awareness."},{sanskrit:"किल्बिषमोषिणि",meaning:"Purification of the heart and mind, wiping away toxic mental impressions (Samskaras) that keep us bound to negative habits."},{sanskrit:"घोषरते",meaning:"Facing the loud, roaring, and chaotic challenges of the material world head-on with celebratory confidence rather than fear."},{sanskrit:"दनुजनिरोषिणि",meaning:"The Danavas (born of Danu) symbolize cosmic inertia, heavy ignorance (Tamas), and deep-seated delusions that cloud clear human judgment."},{sanskrit:"दितिसुतरोषिणि",meaning:"The Daityas symbolize dualism, division, rigid ambition, and the aggressive ego that falsely views the self as completely separate from the Divine."},{sanskrit:"दुर्मदशोषिणि",meaning:"The total deflation of toxic egoism, arrogance, and the false sense of personal doership that blocks spiritual awakening."},{sanskrit:"सिन्धुसुते",meaning:"Goddess Lakshmi, representing the absolute churn of consciousness that ultimately yields boundless peace, spiritual wealth, and divine prosperity."}]},s={slug:"verse-3",title:"Verse 3 : अयि जगदम्ब",description:"3. Our Maa Durga dwells in Kadamba Forest, snow peaked Himalayas. Destroys Madhu and Kaitabha",sanskrit:`
            अयि जगदम्ब मदम्ब कदम्ब वनप्रियवासिनि हासरते
            शिखरि शिरोमणि तुङ्गहिमलय शृङ्गनिजालय मध्यगते ।
            मधुमधुरे मधुकैटभगञ्जिनि कैटभभञ्जिनि रासरते
            जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥ ३ ॥                   
`,meaning:['<strong>3.1:</strong> O <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जगदम्ब">Mother of the Universe</span>, my very own Mother! Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="मदम्ब"> resides intimately as my Mother</span>; Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="कदम्ब वनप्रियवासिनि">loves to dwell in the beautiful Kadamba forest</span>, and Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="हासरते">delights in a joyful, radiant smile</span>. <br />',"<strong>Psychological:</strong> This represents our baseline state of pure mental well-being—a lush, inner landscape where our thoughts are aligned, emotional peace is abundant, and our mind rests in a state of joy, completely undisturbed by the noise of the external world.",'<strong>3.2:</strong> Whose true home is <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शिखरि शिरोमणि">midst the crest-jewel of mountains</span>, resting upon the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="तुङ्गहिमलय शृङ्ग">lofty, snow-capped peaks of the majestic Himalayas</span>.<br />',"<strong>Psychological:</strong> The mountain peak represents the ultimate heights of human awareness and clarity. Just like a high mountain peak sits far above storm clouds, this symbolizes rising above the daily mental chatter and survival states into a cool, calm, and completely unshakeable perspective.",'<strong>3.3:</strong> Who is <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="मधुमधुरे">sweeter than honey itself</span>, yet turned into the fierce <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="मधुकैटभगञ्जिनि">crusher of the demon Madhu</span>, the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="कैटभभञ्जिनि">shatterer of the demon Kaitabha</span>, and Who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रासरते">delights in the divine cosmic dance</span>.<br />',"<strong>Psychological:</strong> When inevitable physical or situational challenges hit us, we must protect ourselves swiftly and decisively slaying Madhu (the craving for comfort and sweetness) without losing our inner sweetness and turning us bitter, while simultaneously conquering Kaitabha (the frantic energy of aversion and hostility). By channeling Durga's sharp, fearless action while dissolving the internal resistance of Madhu and Kaitabha, we neutralize the threat cleanly and keep our inner self light enough to keep dancing through life.",'<strong>3.4:</strong> Victory, victory to You, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="महिषासुरमर्दिनि">O Destroyer of the monstrous demon Mahishasura</span>; You who possess <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि">beautiful, intricately woven locks of hair</span>, and Who are the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते">Daughter of the Mountain</span>.<br />',"<strong>Psychological:</strong> Victory to the inner power of awareness that slays the victim mindset and heavy rumination. By keeping the vast, complex forces of our mind ordered and beautifully contained like bound hair, we securely anchor ourselves back onto the rock-solid mountain of steady mental equilibrium."],symbols:["🌸 Verse 3: The Descent of Divine Wisdom","Jagadamba / Madamba: This establishes the Principle of Divine Proximity. Before showing her terrifying warrior form, the seeker recognizes that the ultimate cosmic power ruling the galaxy is simultaneously as intimate, protective, and loving as one's biological mother.","Kadamba Vana Priya Vasini: Represents the Abundance of the Heart. Forests symbolize dense, wild, tangled human thoughts. Devi dwelling inside it means the Divine resides within the chaotic jungle of our minds to bring organic order, beauty, and life.","Hasarate / Rasarate: Represents Equanimity and Universal Harmony. Even when facing catastrophic wars, the Mother is smiling. To the Divine, the entire creation and destruction of universes is a joyful play (Lila) or a rhythmic dance (Rasa).","Himalaya Shringa: Symbolizes the Highest State of Human Consciousness (Sahasrara Chakra). Snow represents absolute purity, stillness, and untainted clarity. True spiritual power is found when the mind rises above worldly noise into unshakeable focus.",'Demon Madhu:  Derived from the word for "honey" or sweetness. It represents raga—our intense attachment to sensory pleasures, praise, and material comforts.',"Demon Kaitabha: Derived from words associated with sting, insects, or deceit. It represents dvesha—our intense dislike, anger, resistance, and aversion toward unpleasant things or criticism.","Ramya Kapardini: Symbolizes Beautiful Locks of Hair. The intertwined braids represent the complex, woven forces of time, space, and cosmic energy (Prakriti) beautifully bound and controlled under divine command."],philosophy:["<strong>Samatvam (समत्वम्): Equanimity:</strong> Like nature abiding in effortless balance, our true nature is peace. Attachment and Aversion, symbolized by Madhu and Kaitabha, arise from the play of the gunas and pull the mind into conflict and restlessness. When attachment and aversion arise, the mind becomes entangled in resistance and craving. Maa Durga's grace will dissolve these inner demons, helping us return to our original stillness and respond to every situation with equanimity and sweetness."],reflection:["Philosophy Breakdown","The Kadamba Forest and Himalayan Peaks represent a mind flooded with dopamine, serotonin, and oxytocin—a state of undisturbed, peaceful equilibrium. The lush, scenic landscapes of the vibrant Kadamba forest and the serene Himalayan peaks—symbolize a calm, peaceful mind where our neurochemistry is balanced and our 'happy hormones' dance freely.","The twin demons Madhu and Kaitabha namely Raga and Dvesha are the binary psychological conditioning that binds human consciousness to worldly suffering.",'Yet, life inevitably introduces friction, pain, illness, or interpersonal conflict. Raga—our intense attachment to sensory pleasures, praise, and material comforts  while Dvesha—our intense dislike, anger, resistance, and aversion toward unpleasant things or criticism makes such events look like demons, Together, they represent how the human mind constantly oscillates between chasing what feels "sweet" and violently rejecting what feels "bitter," which clouds spiritual clarity..',"Our inner Durga awakens when we consciously choose to fight the secondary suffering caused by Madhu and Kaitabha: the cortisol-spiking victim stories, the endless rumination, and the mental loops that scream 'this is unfair!' ","To handle a crisis like 'Madhu' (honey) means to protect ourselves cleanly from further harm—whether that means visiting a doctor to treat a physical pain, or holding a stern, polite boundary with a difficult family member. We take decisive action on the outside while refusing to let the chaos penetrate our deeper emotional core. By managing the external reality without absorbing the toxicity, we stop the cortisol storm before it starts, allowing our mind to return to its natural, scenic state of peace."],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse3.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/pntverse3.png",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"अयि",meaning:"O! / Hail!"},{sanskrit:"जगदम्ब",meaning:"Mother of the universe"},{sanskrit:"मदम्ब",meaning:"My own mother"},{sanskrit:"कदम्ब-वन-प्रिय-वासिनि",meaning:"Residing in the forest of Kadamba trees"},{sanskrit:"हासरते",meaning:"Delighting in smiling/joy"},{sanskrit:"शिखरि-शिरोमणि",meaning:"Crest-jewel of mountains"},{sanskrit:"तुङ्ग-हिमालय",meaning:"Lofty Himalayas"},{sanskrit:"शृङ्ग-निजालय",meaning:"Residing on the peak"},{sanskrit:"मध्यगते",meaning:"Dwelling in the center"},{sanskrit:"मधु-मधुरे",meaning:"Sweet as honey"},{sanskrit:"मधु-कैटभ-गञ्जिनि",meaning:"Subduer of Madhu and Kaitabha"},{sanskrit:"कैटभ-भञ्जिनि",meaning:"Slayer of Kaitabha"},{sanskrit:"रासरते",meaning:"Delighting in the cosmic dance"},{sanskrit:"जय जय हे",meaning:"Victory, victory to You!"},{sanskrit:"महिषासुर-मर्दििन",meaning:"Slayer of Mahishasura"},{sanskrit:"रम्य-कपर्दिनि",meaning:"With beautiful braided hair"},{sanskrit:"शैलसुते",meaning:"Daughter of the mountain"}]},i={slug:"verse-4",title:"Verse 4: अयि शतखण्ड",description:"4. Maa Durga - Destroyer of Chanda(Ego-Driven Chase) and Munda(Reactive Withdrawal)",sanskrit:`
        अयि शतखण्डविखण्डितरुण्डवितुण्डितशुण्डगजाधिपते ।
        रिपुगजगण्डविदारणचण्डपराक्रमशुण्डमृगाधिपते ॥
        निजभुजदण्डनिपातितखण्डविपातितमुण्डभटाधिपते ।
        जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥  
    `,meaning:['<strong>4.1:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शतखण्ड"> who cuts the enemy into a 100 pieces </span>, into <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="विखण्डितरुण्ड"> tiny atoms </span>; <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="वितुण्डित"> demolishes the enemy to the depths of doom </span>, and who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शुण्द गजाधिपते"> destroys Shunda and his army of elephants </span>. <br />','<strong>4.2:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रिपुगजगण्ड"> who smashes the temples of the enemy’s elephants </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="विदारणचण्ड"> who scatters the army of demon Chanda </span>; <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="पराक्रमशुण्ड"> who displays mighty valour against demon Sunda </span>, and whose <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="मृगाधिपते"> lion‑vehicle strikes Munda’s forehead and destroys his elephant army </span>. <br />','<strong>4.3:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="निजभुजदण्ड"> who with Her own arms delivers punishment </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="निपातितखण्ड"> who hurls endless divine weapons </span>; <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="विपातितमुण्ड भटाधिपते"> who wields a brilliant sword like lightning and destroys Chanda & Munda along with their generals </span>. <br />','<strong>4.4:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि"> Victory, victory to You, Slayer of Mahishasura </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि"> O Beautifully‑haired One </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते"> O Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati) </span>. <br />'],symbols:["[HEADER]  Kali fought and decapitated both the Daityas Chanda and Munda, the two generals of Shumbh and Nisumbh . Because she slew Chanda and Munda, the Goddess earned the name Chamunda.","[BULLET] Chanda (Fierceness/Passion): Symbolizes the flickering, unstable, and unpredictable nature of the mind. It represents the ego's outward, passionate pursuit of fleeting worldly desires.","[BULLET] Munda (Shaved Head/Senseless Withdrawal): Symbolizes a negative state of withdrawal or vairagya (dispassion). Unlike genuine spiritual detachment, Munda represents a reactive closing-off due to hurt, ignorance, or depression.","[BULLET] Chamunda (Transcendence): The Goddess Chamunda represents the supreme spiritual awareness required to defeat these inner tendencies. By invoking this energy within, practitioners transcend the traps of fickle desires and ignorant withdrawal, ultimately achieving spiritual liberation (Siddhatva)","","[SOURCE] 1. Devi Mahatmya: <a href='https://devimahatmya.com' target='_blank'>\"Understanding the Devi Mahatmya's Esoteric Hidden Meaning: Knowing Chanda and Munda\"</a> — devimahatmya.com","[SOURCE] 2. Amar Chitra Katha: <a href='https://amarchitrakatha.com' target='_blank'>\"Durga’s Battle With Shumbha\"</a> — amarchitrakatha.com","[SOURCE] 3. Siddhapedia: <a href='https://siddhapedia.com/chamunda-devi' target='_blank'>\"Chamunda Devi\"</a> — siddhapedia.com"],philosophy:["<strong>Vairāgya:</strong> Chanda Vritti (Ego-Driven Chase) and Munda Vritti (Ignorant/Reactive Withdrawal): Both happiness and sadness arising from the Chanda and Munda Vṛtti are temporary states of mind. They must be observed objectively with true detachment (Vairāgya), rather than avoided through reactive withdrawal."],reflection:["Chanda Vritti (Ego-Driven Chase): Desires for material goods or relationships become destructive when they are fueled by the ego. Instead of you owning the desire, the desire begins to own you, forcing you into an endless, compulsive chase.","Munda Vritti (Ignorant/Reactive Withdrawal): When these ego-driven desires fail, a person might reactively withdraw from the world—such as becoming a withdrawn person simply to escape pain and avoid sadness","The Core Philosophical Lesson: True spiritual growth does not mean running away from life's challenges. Both happiness and sadness arising from the Chanda and Munda Vṛtti are temporary states of mind. They must be observed objectively with true detachment (Vairāgya), rather than avoided through reactive withdrawal.","<strong>Durga reveals that both desire and withdrawal — happiness and sadness alike — are fleeting states, to be transcended through true detachment (Vairāgya).</strong>"],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse4.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/maaverse4.png",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"अयि",meaning:"O! / Hail!"},{sanskrit:"शतखण्ड",meaning:"Cuts the enemy into a hundred pieces"},{sanskrit:"विखण्डितरुण्ड",meaning:"Pulverizes into tiny atoms"},{sanskrit:"वितुण्डित",meaning:"Demolishes the enemy to the depths of doom"},{sanskrit:"शुण्ड गजाधिपते",meaning:"Destroys Shunda and his army of elephants"},{sanskrit:"रिपुगजगण्ड",meaning:"Smashes the temples of the enemy’s elephants"},{sanskrit:"विदारणचण्ड",meaning:"Scatters the army of demon Chanda"},{sanskrit:"पराक्रमशुण्ड",meaning:"Displays mighty valour against demon Sunda"},{sanskrit:"मृगाधिपते",meaning:"Lion‑vehicle strikes Munda’s forehead and destroys his elephant army"},{sanskrit:"निजभुजदण्ड",meaning:"With Her own arms delivers punishment"},{sanskrit:"निपातितखण्ड",meaning:"Endless flow of divine weapons"},{sanskrit:"विपातितमुण्ड भटाधिपते",meaning:"Wields a brilliant sword like lightning, destroys Chanda & Munda and their generals"},{sanskrit:"जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि",meaning:"Victory, victory to You, Slayer of Mahishasura"},{sanskrit:"रम्यकपर्दिनि",meaning:"O Beautifully‑haired One"},{sanskrit:"शैलसुते",meaning:"O Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati)"}]},r={slug:"verse-5",title:"Verse 5: अयि रणदुर्मद",description:"5. Maa Durga - Made Lord Shiva the Messenger against Shumbha and Nisumbha",sanskrit:`
        अयि रणदुर्मद शत्रुवधोदित दुर्धरनिर्जर शक्तिभृते ।
        चतुरविचार धुरीणमहाशिव दूतकृत प्रमथाधिपते ॥
        दुरितदुरीह दुराशयदुर्मति दानवदूत कृतान्तमते ।
        जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥ ५ ॥
    `,meaning:['<strong>5.1:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रणदुर्मद"> who is invincible</span> and <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शत्रुवधोदित">rises to slay the arrogant enemies with evil intentions </span>, and <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दुर्धरनिर्जर"> who mercilessly eliminates evil forces</span>with her<span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शक्तिभृते">  infinite strength </span>. <br />','<strong>5.2:</strong> O Divine Mother, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="चतुरविचारधुरीणमहाशिवदूतकृतप्रमथाधिपते">who made Great Shiva—the chief of the Pramatha ganas and the foremost expert in clever diplomacy—Your messenger</span>.<br />','<strong>5.3:</strong> O Divine Mother, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दुरितदुरीहदुराशयदुर्मतिदानवदूतकृतान्तमते">who completely put an end to the evil designs, wicked motives, and malicious minds of the demon messengers</span>.<br />','<strong>5.4:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि"> Victory, victory to You, O Slayer of Mahishasura </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि"> O Beautiful-haired One </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते"> O Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati) </span>. <br />'],symbols:["[HEADER] The Ultimatum: During the fierce battle against the demon Mahishasura, the demons sent their own messenger to deliver a threat or proposal to the Goddess.The Strategic Choice: In a brilliant counter-move, Goddess Durga appointed Lord Shiva (the supreme Lord of the Pramathas) as her messenger. Shiva is described as Chaturvichara-dhurina (foremost in the art of clever thinking, diplomacy, and discriminating between right and wrong).The Result: Shiva skillfully delivered the Goddess's ultimatum to the demon forces, effectively putting the enemy messenger's sinful proposals to an end.","[BULLET] The Demon Messengers: Your overthinking, anxieties, and intrusive thoughts trying to threaten your peace.","[BULLET] Invoking the Goddess: Surrendering the mental battle and tapping into your inner strength and life force.","[BULLET] Shiva as Messenger: Your higher consciousness taking control with calm, strategic stillness.","[BULLET] The Core Lesson: Stop rushing to fix or fight every single anxious thought. Step back, rest, and let your higher awareness neutralize the noise naturally."],philosophy:["<strong>Sakshi:</strong>In the context of mindfulness, this verse represents the shift from reaction to observation. The demon messengers are our ruminations and anxious thoughts. When we invoke our inner power (Shakti) and surrender the struggle, our higher consciousness (Shiva) handles the situation with stillness and diplomacy. It teaches us that not every intrusive thought requires a reaction. By stepping back, we allow a deeper cosmic intelligence to neutralize the mental noise, choosing restful detachment over impulsive action."],reflection:["This verse beautifully symbolizes the management of overthinking. When our intrusive thoughts act like threatening demon messengers, we often rush to react, which only fuels the anxiety.","By surrendering these thoughts to a higher, inner power—our Divine Feminine energy or life force—we allow our higher consciousness (Shiva) to step in.","Shiva, representing wise deliberation and stillness, acts as the ultimate diplomat. Instead of reactively fighting every thought, we tap into a deeper universal intelligence that resolves the inner conflict for us, reminding us to rest rather than rush into action.","********","Quoted below is the passage about the <strong>Lord Shiva</strong> (verbatim from <a>https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZTp013xAjk/)</a>: ",'<strong style="color: red;">The Universe meets you at the depth of your surrender and not at the height of your struggle</strong>','"We often believe that life rewards only those who struggle the hardest. Yet many of life’s deepest blessings arrive not through force, but through alignment. Surrender does not mean giving up; it means letting go of the illusion that we can control everything. Just as a river reaches the ocean by flowing rather than fighting, life begins to unfold with greater ease when we stop resisting reality and learn to move with Dharma.',"Lord Shiva embodies this wisdom. He reminds us that true strength is not found in domination but in inner stillness. Trying to correlate with this Reel picturization, The sky represents the limitless consciousness that remains untouched by the changing clouds of success, failure, joy, and sorrow. When we surrender our attachment to outcomes while continuing to perform our duties, we discover a peace that struggle alone can never provide.",'From a Dharmic standpoiny, surrender is Śaraṇāgati - trusting the Divine order while fulfilling our responsibilities. We continue to act, serve, love, and strive, but without the burden of trying to control every result. The ego struggles endlessly; the soul surrenders and moves forward. And often, it is in that sacred space of trust that grace enters, doors open, and life reveals a path greater than anything we could have forced into existence." <strong style="color: red;">- Love, Servant of Shiva, thebluehuman</strong> '],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse5.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/maaverse5.png",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"अयि",meaning:"O! / Hail!"},{sanskrit:"रणदुर्मद",meaning:"Those intoxicated with the pride of battle"},{sanskrit:"शत्रुवधोदित",meaning:"Risen or manifested to destroy the enemies"},{sanskrit:"दुर्धरनिर्जर",meaning:"Irresistible, invincible, and immortal"},{sanskrit:"शक्तिभृते",meaning:"Bearer of divine power / Wielder of supreme energy"},{sanskrit:"चतुरविचार धुरीण",meaning:"Foremost and highly skilled in clever diplomacy and wise deliberation"},{sanskrit:"महाशिव",meaning:"The Great Lord Shiva"},{sanskrit:"दूतकृत",meaning:"Who was appointed as a messenger"},{sanskrit:"प्रमथाधिपते",meaning:"The master and commander of the Pramatha attendants (Shiva's ganas)"},{sanskrit:"दुरितदुरीह",meaning:"Sinful efforts and wicked designs"},{sanskrit:"दुराशयदुर्मति",meaning:"Evil motives and malicious minds"},{sanskrit:"दानवदूत",meaning:"The messenger of the demons"},{sanskrit:"कृतान्तमते",meaning:"Who put an absolute end to (acted as the destroyer of) their intent"},{sanskrit:"जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि",meaning:"Victory, victory to You, Slayer of Mahishasura"},{sanskrit:"रम्यकपर्दिनि",meaning:"With beautiful locks of hair"},{sanskrit:"शैलसुते",meaning:"O Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati)"}]},o={slug:"verse-6",title:"Verse 6: अयि शरणागत",description:"6. Maa Durga - She Showed Compassion Towards Those Who Surrender",sanskrit:`
        अयि शरणागत वैरिवधुवर वीरवराभय दायकरे ।
        त्रिभुवनमस्तक शूलविरोधि शिरोऽधिकृतामल शूलकरे ॥
        दुमिदुमितामर धुन्दुभिनादमहोमुखरीकृत दिङ्मकरे ।
        जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥ ६ ॥
    `,meaning:['<strong>6.1:</strong> O Divine Mother, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शरणागत">who grants refuge to those who surrender</span>, and <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="वैरिवधुवर वीरवराभय दायकरे">bestows fearlessness even upon the noble wives and heroic warriors of Your enemies when they seek Your protection</span>.<br />','<strong>6.2:</strong> O Divine Mother, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="त्रिभुवनमस्तक">whose power governs the three worlds</span>, and <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="अमल शूलकरे">who wields the pure trident</span> that destroys all opposition to Divine Truth.<br />','<strong>6.3:</strong> O Divine Mother, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="दुमिदुमितामर धुन्दुभिनाद">whose victory is celebrated by the resounding celestial drums</span>, filling all directions with divine sound and glory.<br />','<strong>6.4:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि"> Victory, victory to You, O Slayer of Mahishasura </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि"> O Beautiful-haired One </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते"> O Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati) </span>. <br />'],symbols:["[HEADER] The Compassionate Victor: Even in the midst of battle, the Goddess protects those who sincerely surrender. Her victory is not driven by hatred but by Dharma.","[BULLET] Enemy Wives Seeking Refuge: The vulnerable parts of ourselves that seek healing after destructive patterns collapse.","[BULLET] Abhaya (Fearlessness): Inner security that arises when we surrender to truth rather than ego.","[BULLET] The Trident (Trishula): The power of wisdom that pierces ignorance across body, mind, and intellect.","[BULLET] Celestial Drums: The inner joy and clarity that naturally arise when a major inner conflict is resolved.","[BULLET] The Core Lesson: True strength is not merely defeating an enemy; it is remaining compassionate even after victory."],philosophy:["<strong>Dharmic principle:</strong> Divine power is always balanced by Divine compassion. Durga destroys harmful tendencies, but she offers protection to anyone who sincerely seeks refuge, even those associated with the opposing side. Spiritually, the verse teaches that once ego-driven tendencies are defeated, we should not continue fighting remnants of the past with bitterness. Instead, wisdom must be accompanied by compassion. The sound of the celestial drums symbolizes the harmony that emerges when justice and mercy work together."],reflection:["Many people believe strength means never showing compassion toward those who have wronged them. This verse presents a higher ideal. Durga defeats evil decisively, yet grants protection to those who sincerely surrender.","In our inner life, destructive habits, fears, and ego-driven desires often create suffering. When these tendencies weaken, the goal is not self-hatred or endless punishment. Instead, we acknowledge the lesson, learn from it, and move forward with wisdom.","The Goddess reminds us that true power includes both firmness and mercy. We can uphold boundaries without losing compassion.","********",'<strong style="color: red;">Compassion is not weakness. It is the strength that remains after the battle has already been won.</strong>',"Just as Durga's victory drums echo throughout the cosmos, inner peace naturally resonates through our lives when courage and compassion become one."],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse6.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/maaverse6.png",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"अयि",meaning:"O Divine Mother!"},{sanskrit:"शरणागत",meaning:"Those who have sought refuge"},{sanskrit:"वैरिवधुवर",meaning:"The noble wives of enemies"},{sanskrit:"वीरवर",meaning:"Great heroes and warriors"},{sanskrit:"अभय दायकरे",meaning:"Bestower of fearlessness and protection"},{sanskrit:"त्रिभुवन",meaning:"The three worlds"},{sanskrit:"मस्तक",meaning:"Head, chief, ruler"},{sanskrit:"शूलविरोधि",meaning:"Those opposing the divine trident"},{sanskrit:"अमल शूलकरे",meaning:"Bearer of the pure and spotless trident"},{sanskrit:"दुमिदुमित",meaning:"Resounding repeatedly"},{sanskrit:"अमर",meaning:"The celestial beings"},{sanskrit:"धुन्दुभिनाद",meaning:"Sound of the heavenly drums"},{sanskrit:"दिङ्मकरे",meaning:"Filling all directions"},{sanskrit:"जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि",meaning:"Victory, victory to You, Slayer of Mahishasura"},{sanskrit:"रम्यकपर्दिनि",meaning:"The One with beautiful locks of hair"},{sanskrit:"शैलसुते",meaning:"Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati)"}]},h={slug:"verse-7",title:"Verse 7: अयि निजहुङ्कृति मात्र",description:"7. Maa Durga - Destroyed Dhumralochana , Rakthabeeja, Shumbha-Nishumbha ",sanskrit:`
        अयि निजहुङ्कृति मात्रनिराकृत धूम्रविलोचन धूम्रशते ।
        समरविशोषित शोणितबीज समुद्भवशोणित बीजलते ॥
        शिवशिवशुम्भ निशुम्भमहाहव तर्पितभूत पिशाचरते ।
        जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥ ७ ॥
    `,meaning:['<strong>7.1:</strong> O Divine Mother, who by a mere sound of <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="निजहुङ्कृति">Your sacred roar </span> <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="मात्रनिराकृत">destroyed</span> the demon <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="धूम्रविलोचन">Dhumralochana</span>into <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="धूम्रशते">hundred smoke particles</span>.<br />','<strong>7.2:</strong> O Mother, who <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="समरविशोषित">dried up</span> the battlefield where <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शोणितबीज">Raktabīja</span> and his <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="समुद्भवशोणित बीजलते">countless blood-born duplicates arose</span, ending the endless cycle of replication of ignorance and desire.<br />','<strong>7.3:</strong> O Divine One, who in the Great Auspicious Sacrifice (resembling a Yagna)  of <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शुम्भ-निशुम्भ">Shumbha and Nishumbha</span> <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="तर्पितभूत पिशाचरते">satisfied the thirst of Ghosts and Fiends (attending Lord Shiva)</span>, restoring cosmic balance through righteous battle.<br />','<strong>7.4:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि"> Victory, victory to You, O Slayer of Mahishasura </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि"> O Beautiful-haired One </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते"> O Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati) </span>. <br />'],symbols:["[HEADER] The Destruction of Dhumralochana (Inner Delusion)","[BULLET] Nijahunkriti (Sacred Roar): The power of inner awareness that instantly dissolves confusion and fear.",'[BULLET] Dhumralochana: The "smoky-eyed" demon represents ignorance, confusion, and a clouded vision of reality. The Mother does not need physical weapons to destroy this mental fog; a single stroke of divine sound or inner awareness (Humkara) completely shatters the deluding forces that obscure truth..',"[HEADER] The Drying up of Raktabīja (Endless Desires)","[BULLET] Raktabīja Symbolism: The endless multiplication of thoughts, desires, and anxieties when fed by attention.Raktabīja represents our desires, negative thoughts, and egoistic tendencies. Every time one desire is met or struck down, a drop falls and multiple new cravings sprout up in its place. The Divine Mother stops this exhausting, endless cycle by completely consuming and drying up the root source of desire within the seeker.","[HEADER] The War of Shumbha and Nishumbha (Cosmic Alignment)",'[BULLET] Battlefield Drying: The stopping of emotional reactivity that fuels repetitive mental suffering.Shumbha and Nishumbha represent the deep-seated dualities of "I-ness" (attachment to the individual self) and "My-ness" (attachment to possessions). By destroying them, the Mother feeds and satisfies the elemental, transformative forces of the cosmos, channeling primal energy back into a state of pure, righteous balance (Shiva-Shiva)',"[HEADER] The Ultimate Salutation (The Auspicious Mother)","[BULLET] Core Lesson: When awareness is steady, reactive thought-proliferation collapses on its own. Even amidst the most intense cosmic warfare, the Mother remains eternally beautiful, composed, and unshakeable. Her mountain lineage symbolizes her eternal, immovable, and foundational presence as the source of all existence."],philosophy:["<strong>Chitta Vritti Nirodha (निरोध):</strong>The demons are no longer external forces but symbolic of mental patterns: confusion (Dhumralochana) and self-replicating thoughts (Raktabīja). The Goddess does not fight them endlessly; instead, her vibration alone dissolves their power. This reflects the principle that awareness itself is transformative. When consciousness becomes steady and non-reactive, the compulsive multiplication of thoughts stops naturally without force."],reflection:["This verse speaks directly to the experience of overthinking. Some thoughts don’t need to be solved—they need to be witnessed.","The more attention we give to repetitive fears or desires, the more they multiply, like Raktabīja's endless forms.","Durga’s roar represents a shift in awareness where you stop feeding mental noise with reaction.",'<strong style="color: red;">What you resist in the mind multiplies. What you witness dissolves.</strong>',"Silence is not emptiness—it is the moment when the mind stops reproducing its own disturbances.","********","Quoted below is the passage about the <strong>Lord Shiva</strong> (verbatim from <a>https://www.artofliving.org/in-en/navratri/the-end-of-evil)</a> and <a>https://www.facebook.com/watch/?v=1226149297784868</a>: ","The Story: The brothers performed severe penance to become invincible. After conquering the heavens, they encountered Goddess Durga. Smitten by her beauty, they demanded she marry them. She declared she would only marry whoever could defeat her in battle, ultimately destroying both of them and their armies","In spiritual philosophy, their characters represent negative psychological states that destroy inner peace:",'Shumbha: Symbolizes ego ("I and mine") and the inflated sense of self that blinds a person to humility.',"Nishumbha: Symbolizes self-doubt and the continuous doubting of others, as well as the insatiable craving for validation."],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse7.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/pntverse7.png",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"अयि",meaning:"O Mother!"},{sanskrit:"निजहुङ्कृति",meaning:"By Your own sacred roar"},{sanskrit:"मात्रनिराकृत",meaning:"Destroyed effortlessly"},{sanskrit:"धूम्रविलोचन",meaning:"The smoky-eyed demon Dhumralochana"},{sanskrit:"समरविशोषित",meaning:"Dried up in battle"},{sanskrit:"शोणितबीज",meaning:"Raktabīja, the blood-seed demon"},{sanskrit:"समुद्भवशोणित",meaning:"From whose blood countless forms arise"},{sanskrit:"बीजलते",meaning:"Endless chain of self-reproduction"},{sanskrit:"शिवशिवशुम्भ",meaning:"Auspicious destruction of Shumbha"},{sanskrit:"निशुम्भमहाहव",meaning:"In the great battle with Nishumbha"},{sanskrit:"तर्पितभूत पिशाचरते",meaning:"Satisfying and subduing all spirits and forces of chaos"},{sanskrit:"जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि",meaning:"Victory, victory to You, Slayer of Mahishasura"},{sanskrit:"रम्यकपर्दिनि",meaning:"Beautiful-haired One"},{sanskrit:"शैलसुते",meaning:"Daughter of the Mountain"}]},l={slug:"verse-8",title:"Verse 8: धनुरनुषङ्ग",description:"8. Maa Durga - Destroyed the Chaturanga [Daily Challenges] ",sanskrit:`
        धनुरनुषङ्ग रणक्षणसङ्ग परिस्फुरदङ्ग नटत्कटके
        कनकपिशङ्ग पृषत्कनिषङ्ग रसद्भटशृङ्ग हताबटुके ।
        कृतचतुरङ्ग बलक्षितिरङ्ग घटद्बहुरङ्ग रटद्बटुके
        जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि रम्यकपर्दिनि शैलसुते ॥ ८ ॥
    `,meaning:['<strong>8.1:</strong> O Divine Mother, who is <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रणक्षणसङ्ग">deeply engaged in battle at every moment</span> with intense <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="परिस्फुरदङ्ग">cosmic energy quivering through Your limbs</span>, and whose <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="नटत्कटके">bracelets appear to dance on Your vibrant arms</span> following the swift, continuous <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="धनुरनुषङ्ग">stringing movements of Your bow</span>.<br />','<strong>8.2:</strong> O Divine Mother, whose <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="कनकपिशङ्ग">golden arrows shine with a reddish hue</span> as they are launched from Your <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="पृषत्कनिषङ्ग">brilliant quiver packed with arrows</span>, smiting down the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रसद्भटशृङ्ग">vain pride of roaring enemy warriors</span> and <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="हताबटुके">completely destroying them in battle</span>.<br />','<strong>8.3:</strong> O Divine Mother, who faces the <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="कृतचतुरङ्ग">fourfold traditional army of the enemy</span> to break their <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="बलक्षितिरङ्ग">strength on the playground of the battlefield</span>, creating an expansive <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="घटद्बहुरङ्ग">drama of diverse divine plays</span> where Your <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रटद्बटुके">shouting spirit-clans and young attendants echo Your glory</span>.<br />','<strong>8.4:</strong> O Divine Mother <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="जय जय हे महिषासुरमर्दिनि"> Victory, victory to You, O Slayer of Mahishasura </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="रम्यकपर्दिनि"> O Beautiful-haired One </span>, <span class="highlight" data-sanskrit="शैलसुते"> O Daughter of the Mountain (Parvati) </span>. <br />'],symbols:["[HEADER] The Four-Fold Army (Chaturanga):","[BULLET] Representing the four divisions of an ancient army (chariots, elephants, cavalry, and infantry), this symbolizes the sheer, overwhelming force of ignorance, ego, and worldly attachments. It signifies that Durga fights against all conceivable odds and directions of worldly illusion (Maya).","[HEADER] The Dancing Bracelet","[BULLET]  When a person fights, a trembling or dancing bracelet conveys delicate grace and lightness. In this context, it shows that despite the intense, catastrophic scale of the cosmic battle, Durga is not struggling. The battle does not disturb her inner tranquility.","[HEADER] Destruction as Cosmic Play (Lila)","[BULLET]  The dancing ornament reveals that for the Supreme Consciousness, annihilating negativity is not a grueling physical struggle, but a graceful, rhythmic dance. It demonstrates supreme mastery over time, action, and the material universe.","[HEADER] Fierce yet Maternal Grace",'[BULLET]  As a warrior, Durga is terrifying to the forces of chaos. However, the "dancing" of her jewelry reminds devotees that she is ultimately the nurturing Mother. She destroys evil only to protect the cosmos, restoring peace and righteousness with absolute elegance.'],philosophy:['<strong>Sthitaprajna (स्थितप्रज्ञ):</strong>When the daily Chaturanga of life attacks - <strong>1.</strong> Tune into your inner Shakti during stressful moments, shift from panicked reaction to centered action by anchoring your mind and body. , <strong>2.</strong> Practice the Chandi Breath—inhaling deep strength and exhaling tension with a powerful, clearing "Haa" release.<strong>3.</strong> Maintain the poise of the Dancing Bracelet by consciously softening your jaw and shoulders with an upright posture. <strong>4.</strong> Mentally step back to view your stressors as an external army rather than your identity, repeating the grounding mantra, <strong>"I am the weapon, She is the warrior"</strong>.'],reflection:["1. Facing Your Daily Chaturanga","<strong>The Modern Four-Fold Army:</strong> In daily life, Chaturanga manifests as multi-front challenges—work pressure, relationship stress, financial anxiety, and internal self-doubt.","<strong>Constant Attacks:</strong> Just like the ancient army, modern problems attack from multiple directions at once, threatening to overwhelm your mental peace.",'2. The Trap of "Stop Adorning" (The Burnout Phase)','<strong>Losing the Grace:</strong>When hit by life\'s chaos, the human instinct is to drop our "ornaments"—our joy, our calmness, our self-care, and our kindness—believing we only have room to fight. ',"<strong>Becoming the Chaos:</strong>If you fight anxiety with anxiety, or anger with anger, you become identical to the enemy you are fighting, losing your inner grace and strength.</strong>","Fighting Passionately with Grace",'<strong>The Dancing Bracelet Principle: </strong>o "adorn yourself" while fighting means keeping your actions rooted in poise, ethics, and calm clarity, even during intense conflict.',"<strong>Action Without Friction: </strong>You do not escape the battle of life; you meet it head-on with full energy and passion, but your inner core remains completely still, like the calm at the center of a storm.","4. Tuning into Inner Shakti Durga","<strong>Becoming the Weapon: </strong>Surrendering your ego to the Divine Mother means realizing you do not have to carry the heavy burden of the battle alone.","<strong>Divine Guidance: </strong> When you tune into this inner energy, your actions stop being reactionary panic. Instead, they become purposeful, guided movements, turning your daily struggles into a graceful, victorious dance of life.","<Strong>TUNE INTO YOUR INNER SHAKTHI</strong>",'To tune into your inner Shakti during stressful moments, you must shift from panicked reaction to centered action by anchoring your mind and body. When the daily Chaturanga of life attacks, practice the Chandi Breath—inhaling deep strength and exhaling tension with a powerful, clearing "Haa" release. Maintain the poise of the Dancing Bracelet by consciously softening your jaw and shoulders while keeping a regal, upright posture, proving to your nervous system that you remain unbothered by the chaos. Finally, mentally step back to view your stressors as an external army rather than your identity, repeating the grounding mantra, "I am the weapon, She is the warrior." This bio-hacks your neurochemistry, shifting you from a defensive state into a commanding, graceful presence capable of navigating any conflict with absolute tranquility.'],image1:"/images/maa/maaverse8.png",imagepnt:"/images/maa/maaverse8.png",stanzaData:[{sanskrit:"धनुरनुषङ्ग रणक्षणसङ्ग परिस्फुरदङ्ग नटत्कटके",meaning:"<strong> O Divine Mother, who is deeply engaged in battle at every moment with intense cosmic energy quivering through Your limbs, and whose bracelets appear to dance on Your vibrant arms following the swift, continuous stringing movements of Your bow.</strong>"},{sanskrit:"धनुरनुषङ्ग",meaning:"With the continuous stringing and movement of Your bow"},{sanskrit:"रणक्षणसङ्ग",meaning:"Engaged deeply in every single moment of the battle"},{sanskrit:"परिस्फुरदङ्ग",meaning:"Whose divine limbs throb and quiver with intense cosmic energy"},{sanskrit:"नटत्कटके",meaning:"Whose armlets and bracelets appear to dance with the swift movements of war"},{sanskrit:"कनकपिशङ्ग पृषत्कनिषङ्ग रसद्भटशृङ्ग हताबटुके",meaning:"  <strong> O Divine Mother, whose golden arrows shine with a reddish hue as they are launched from Your brilliant quiver packed with arrows, smiting down the vain pride of roaring enemy warriors and completely destroying them in battle.</strong>"},{sanskrit:"कनकपिशङ्ग",meaning:"Shining with a brilliant golden-reddish hue"},{sanskrit:"पृषत्कनिषङ्ग",meaning:"Bearing a quiver filled with bright, pointed arrows"},{sanskrit:"रसद्भटशृङ्ग",meaning:"The heads and pride of the loudly roaring enemy warriors"},{sanskrit:"हताबटुके",meaning:"Smiting down and destroying completely"},{sanskrit:"कृतचतुरङ्ग बलक्षितिरङ्ग घटद्बहुरङ्ग रटद्बटुके",meaning:" <strong> O Divine Mother, who faces the fourfold traditional army of the enemy to break their strength on the playground of the battlefield, creating an expansive drama of diverse divine plays where Your shouting spirit-clans and young attendants echo Your glory.</strong>"},{sanskrit:"कृतचतुरङ्ग",meaning:"Facing the fourfold traditional army (elephants, chariots, cavalry, infantry)"},{sanskrit:"बलक्षितिरङ्ग",meaning:"Annihilating their strength on the playground of the battlefield"},{sanskrit:"घटद्बहुरङ्ग",meaning:"Creating an expansive drama of multiple expressions and divine plays"},{sanskrit:"रटद्बटुके",meaning:"Where loud, cheering spirit-clans and young ascetic attendants echo Your glory"}]};e.s(["verses",0,{mantra:a,"verse-1":t,"verse-2":n,"verse-3":s,"verse-4":i,"verse-5":r,"verse-6":o,"verse-7":h,"verse-8":l}],3596);var d=e.i(43476),g=e.i(18741);function u({text:e,forPrint:a=!1}){if(!e)return null;let t=Array.isArray(e)?e:e.split("\n");return(0,d.jsxs)("section",{className:a?g.default.print:g.default.reflectionCard,children:[!a&&(0,d.jsx)("h3",{children:"✍️ रक्ष रक्ष जगन्माते सर्वशक्ते जय दुर्गे।🙏"}),(0,d.jsx)("blockquote",{children:t.map((e,a)=>{let t="string"==typeof e?e.trim():"";return t?(0,d.jsx)("p",{children:(0,d.jsx)("span",{dangerouslySetInnerHTML:{__html:t}})},a):null})})]})}e.s(["ReflectionBlock",()=>u],94857)},18566,(e,a,t)=>{a.exports=e.r(76562)}]);